---
import ToolPage from '../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import OnDutyCalculator from '../components/OnDutyCalculator.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What date is Thanksgiving this year and next?',
    a: 'Thanksgiving is the fourth Thursday of November: November 26 in 2026, November 25 in 2027, and November 23 in 2028. Because the date moves within a six-day window each year while your rotation drifts on its own cycle, whether you work it is genuinely unpredictable without calculating.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is working Black Friday the real problem?',
    a: 'Often, yes. Many shift-worker families travel Thursday through Sunday, so a Friday or Saturday duty day cancels the trip just as surely as Thursday would. Generate your calendar and look at the whole four-day window, not just the Thursday.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Thanksgiving falls in my 3-day work run — can I still make dinner?',
    a: 'If you are on 12-hour days, dinner usually moves to 7–8 pm and you arrive tired but present; if you are on nights, the meal happens before your shift starts. The unworkable case is a 24-hour tour, which is why firefighter families are famous for firehouse Thanksgiving — many departments cook the full meal at the station.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I trade away Thanksgiving to protect Christmas?',
    a: 'A classic strategy: volunteer for Thanksgiving early, and bank the goodwill (or the explicit trade) for December 25. It works best on rotations where the two holidays fall to different crews — check both dates above before offering.',
  },
];
---

<ToolPage
  title="Am I Working on Thanksgiving? — Check Your Shift Rotation"
  description="Check in seconds whether your rotating schedule puts you on duty for Thanksgiving — and the whole 4-day weekend. Works for 2-2-3, Pitman, DuPont, 4-on-4-off, 48/96, and Kelly rotations."
  h1="Am I working on Thanksgiving?"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    Thanksgiving is the one holiday whose <em>date itself</em> moves — fourth Thursday of November —
    while your rotation drifts on its own cycle. Enter your pattern and one known workday, and the
    list below answers Thanksgiving and every other federal holiday for the next 12 months, so the
    turkey question is settled before anyone books flights.
  </p>

  <OnDutyCalculator defaultPattern="all" highlightHoliday="Thanksgiving" />

  <h2>Think in four-day weekends, not Thursdays</h2>
  <p>
    For most families the real unit is Thursday-to-Sunday. A rotation can spare you Thanksgiving
    itself and still take Friday and Saturday — which, if your people are three states away, cancels
    the trip anyway. Once your calendar is generated above, read the whole window: on a
    <a href="/2-2-3-panama-schedule-calculator">2-2-3</a> the long weekend either lands entirely in
    your 3-day break (jackpot) or gets split by the Friday–Sunday work run; on
    <a href="/4-on-4-off-schedule-calculator">4-on-4-off</a> the drift means some years hand you all
    four days free without using a single hour of leave.
  </p>

  <h2>The shift worker's Thanksgiving playbook</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Working Thursday only?</strong> Move dinner. Most shift families eat their Thanksgiving
    on the Friday or Saturday inside the off-block — the calculator shows exactly which day that is.
    <strong>Working nights?</strong> The shift that starts Thursday evening still leaves the
    afternoon meal intact; protect your pre-shift sleep by eating early.
    <strong>On a 24-hour tour?</strong> Bring the family to the station if your department allows
    it — firehouse Thanksgiving is a genuine American tradition, not a consolation prize.
    <strong>Off but exhausted?</strong> If Thursday is day one after a night run, warn the cook you
    will be vertical by 2 pm, not 10 am.
  </p>

  <h2>Trade season starts in September</h2>
  <p>
    Thanksgiving trades clear earlier than Christmas ones because travel plans firm up around
    Labor Day. If the calculator shows you on duty, September is when a swap costs a favor and
    November is when it costs a big favor. And before you trade, check
    <a href="/am-i-working-on-christmas">Christmas</a> too — giving away Thanksgiving to a colleague
    who then covers your December 25 is the best deal in shift work, but only if the cycle actually
    assigns you both.
  </p>
</ToolPage>
